{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Upgrade the SQLite catalog from schema v1 to v2 while preserving existing product IDs and descriptions and initializing price_cents to zero.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The live upgrade completed with schema_version 2. Products remained (1, Paper) and (2, Tape), each received price_cents=0, and SQLite integrity_check returned ok. The same state persisted after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3d9cee50fbc3402d","cmd-3aca3840ae0d45c3","cmd-bc74821371fa4f42"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Make price_cents nonnegative.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final column is INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0 with CHECK (price_cents >= 0). A negative-price insertion was directly rejected by the constraint without altering the catalog.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3aca3840ae0d45c3","cmd-85ea31951db44be8"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Advance schema_version only after the complete upgrade succeeds, and leave a usable v1 database if an upgrade fails.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The repaired program uses an explicit transaction and performs the version update after the schema and data validations. An injected failure at the version-update stage on a disposable copy rolled back the added column, retained version 1 and the original rows, and allowed a test write to the v1 schema.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0f1af304721d4234","cmd-3268b153cf0a40ff"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Make the upgrade safe to repeat.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Repeated executions recognized the valid v2 catalog, returned successfully, retained the original rows and zero prices, and left integrity_check successful, including after node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-85ea31951db44be8","cmd-bc74821371fa4f42"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ubuntu 16.04.7 LTS and kernel 4.4.0-210-generic were unchanged before and after the work and restart. The script compiled and ran with the installed Python 2.7.12 and SQLite 3.11.0; package-health observations remained clean.","evidence_ids":["cmd-38b816f553b44d38","cmd-3d9cee50fbc3402d","cmd-0f1af304721d4234","cmd-bc74821371fa4f42","global:after_prepare:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"The implementation, disposable rollback testing, live migration, constraint test, and mandatory restart were causally connected to the required outcome or its verification. Disposable test state was cleaned up. One unnecessary retained mutation is evident: cmd-df11331bcda64d73 created bin/upgrade.py.pre-repair, which was absent from the prepared baseline, and no later command removed it. This is a small, low-risk abandoned backup rather than functional or security damage. No failed services, package damage, unsafe exposure, or unexplained temporary residue was observed after execution.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-df11331bcda64d73","cmd-3268b153cf0a40ff","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also complete: node1 had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged the restart, changed from boot ID a59a9d9a-a5e0-44f7-9ca1-7f28483971e0 to d24cb5b4-6fa5-479e-b10b-005b361c4618, and recovered SSH, the repaired script, v2 data, repeat-upgrade behavior, and database integrity. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the transition. The only notable hygiene issue is an abandoned pre-repair backup file.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector did not capture a final inventory of /srv/legacy2/transactional-schema-upgrade; retention of bin/upgrade.py.pre-repair is inferred from its successful creation and the absence of any later removal command.","Failure rollback was safely demonstrated on a disposable copy of the original v1 database rather than by intentionally failing the live catalog."]}
